# openspec/config.yaml — recommended project config for the change-type schema set.
# Rules are injected only for the matching artifact id; `context` is injected
# into every artifact (capped ~50KB). Note: artifact ids shared across schemas
# (tasks, proposal, migration) share their rules — written below to read
# correctly in every schema that uses the id.

schema: feature   # default when the router is unsure; override with --schema

context: |
  <Fill in the angle-bracket slots before first use - the cheapest quality
  lever in this whole setup.>

  Read ./TOOLS.md before starting work, and again before changing dependencies,
  scripts, build config, test setup, or deploy settings. It is the canonical map
  of this project's stack, commands, and agent capabilities - take package
  manager and command names from it rather than the ones you would reach for by
  default. Where the repo has no TOOLS.md, the mapping-project-tooling skill
  builds one from evidence on disk. Where TOOLS.md and the repo's own manifest or
  lockfile disagree, the repo wins: verify, then correct TOOLS.md in this change.

  Public contracts, never to change silently: <APIs, URLs, analytics events,
  data schemas, config keys>
  Constraints not visible from the code: <what this project cannot use, and why>
  API style and where it is specified: <REST/GraphQL/gRPC; path to the spec>

  Before creating a change, read the schema router (ROUTING.md / AGENTS.md)
  and pass the chosen schema: openspec new change <slug> --schema <schema>.

  On UI work with impeccable installed, leave Anthropic's frontend-design
  skill disabled - two design skills with different vocabularies cancel out.

  Six stores outlive a change; write each fact to its owner: behaviour to
  openspec/specs/, vocabulary to CONTEXT.md, binding decisions to docs/adr/,
  product truth to PRODUCT.md, visual tokens to DESIGN.md (frontmatter
  normative), and stack, commands, and agent capabilities to TOOLS.md.
  Anything inside a change folder archives as history.

  Prefer the real tool over the approximation whenever both exist, and say
  so when a named tool is absent. A dispatched subagent inherits nothing:
  its prompt names every capability it should use. Guidance that must fire
  every time is a hook, not a memory line - memory is advisory and agents
  deviate under pressure.

rules:
  brainstorm:
    - Ask one question at a time; prefer multiple-choice.
    - Record the exact message in which the user approved the intent, so the approval is citable.
    - Propose 2-3 approaches with trade-offs; lead with a recommendation.
    - Present the design in sections and get approval before proceeding.
    - Apply YAGNI; record what is explicitly out of scope.

  constraints:
    - Record the domain's ubiquitous language; align terms to CONTEXT.md if present.
    - List everything that must never change silently (APIs, URLs, analytics events, form fields, schemas).
    - For UI surfaces, audit and extract existing brand/design tokens before proposing changes.

  proposal:
    - Keep the Why between roughly 50 and 1000 characters.
    - Use From/To phrasing for behavioral changes.
    - State impact and the rollback posture.

  specs:
    - Every requirement is observable and testable, with at least one Given/When/Then scenario.
    - Use RFC 2119 keywords (MUST/SHALL/SHOULD/MAY) for requirement strength.
    - A MODIFIED delta replaces the whole requirement - repeat every surviving scenario.
    - Reframe vague asks into measurable criteria (e.g. "fast" -> "LCP < 2.5s on 4G").
    - Reference existing patterns before inventing new ones.
    - When controlled-engineering-english is installed, write requirement prose in its spec register - one glossary term per concept, no SHOULD where the requirement is absolute.

  design:
    - State decisions before implementation.
    - Record an ADR only when a decision is hard to reverse AND surprising without context AND a real trade-off.
    - For UI surfaces, name the surface mode (Persuade/Operate/Read/Experience) and reference DESIGN.md if present.
    - When controlled-engineering-english is installed, ADR prose follows its engineer register.

  migration:
    - Prefer expand/contract for schema and data changes.
    - Specify explicit rollback triggers and the exact rollback procedure; name the point of no return.
    - Classify each removal as compulsory or advisory deprecation, with a timeline.

  review:
    - Review across five axes - correctness, readability/architecture, security, performance, testability - leading with correctness and security.
    - Label findings by severity (Critical / Nit / Optional / FYI).
    - Approve when the change improves overall code health, even if not perfect.
    - Target ~100 changed lines per reviewable unit; ~300 is the ceiling; split anything near ~1000.
    - Screen input, auth, storage, and external integrations against the OWASP Top 10.
    - For UI surfaces, run the anti-slop pre-flight - WCAG AA contrast, no default AI-purple, no em-dash tells, no fake screenshots.

  tasks:
    - Decompose into small, independently verifiable steps with acceptance criteria and dependency order.
    - Keep every step rollback-friendly; each step names how it is verified.
    - Name each task's tooling as "use X when available, otherwise <fallback>" - an executor subagent inherits nothing, so an unnamed tool is an unused one.

  plan:
    - Each step is one 2-5 minute action with exact file paths.
    - TDD iron law - no production code without a failing test first; code written before its test gets deleted and redone.
    - Make the cycle explicit - RED, verify red, GREEN, verify green, REFACTOR, COMMIT.
    - Placeholders are plan failures; write the real test code, the real handling, the repeated code.

  reproduction:
    - Note relevant session capabilities (skills, MCP tools, subagents) before planning; a fresh executor inherits nothing, so name tools in tasks.
    - Write the failing test before any fix; if truly untestable, justify and give a manual verification script.
    - Pin the environment - versions, config, data preconditions.

  rootcause:
    - Trace symptom to faulty assumption (five whys), not just to the faulty line.
    - Scan for the same bug class elsewhere in the codebase; record hits or "none found".

  fix:
    - Prefer the cause layer; a documented symptom fix is a decision, an undocumented one is an accident.
    - Include a spec delta only if the documented contract changes.

  characterization:
    - Pin current behavior with tests before moving any code; record coverage gaps and close them first.

  strategy:
    - Sequence as small reversible steps, tests green and committable after each.
    - No behavior change at any step; a changed test assertion means reclassify to feature.

  question:
    - One primary question per spike, with a hard timebox and the decision it informs.

  findings:
    - Evidence over opinion - measurements and snippets, not impressions.
    - Disposition is mandatory - code discarded; learnings graduate via a new feature change.

  inventory:
    - Note relevant session capabilities before planning; name tools in tasks - a fresh executor inherits nothing.
    - Cite official release notes/changelogs by URL; list each breaking change with an applies-to-us verdict.

  surfaces:
    - Verify usage by search (grep/AST), not memory; give paths and counts per breaking change.
    - Classify each surface - mechanical (codemod), behavioral (needs a test), unknown (spike first).

  triage:
    - Decide rollback vs forward-fix explicitly and name the approver.
    - Keep triage to minutes, not hours - depth belongs in the postmortem.

  postmortem:
    - Blameless; include a timeline and why existing tests/review missed it.
    - Creating the follow-up bugfix/feature change is mandatory; link it.
    - When controlled-engineering-english is installed, write the executive summary in stakeholder register and the technical timeline in engineer register.

  decisions:
    - One question at a time when eliciting stack choices; recommend defaults.
    - At bootstrap most stack choices pass the ADR three-gate test - record them.

  conventions:
    - Write conventions to be copied into standing docs during apply, not summarized.
    - Point AGENTS.md at the project glossary (Glossary: <path>) when controlled-engineering-english is installed.

  verification:
    - Paste command output verbatim; no paraphrased evidence.
    - A run from earlier in the session is not evidence; run the command for this file.
